{
  "summary": "The previous proposal's Tier 1 claim of 'zero-risk, shippable today' is overstated in three ways. First, the line ranges cited are wrong — the proposal says lines 85-490 contain 7 pure functions (~400 lines), but _build_feature_detail_widgets (lines 310-483) is not a pure function: it imports and instantiates Textual widgets (Static, Collapsible), calls Config() and load_scripts(), and thus has side effects and framework dependencies that make it unsuitable for a 'pure helpers' module tested with plain pytest. Extracting it into tui_helpers.py would either pull Textual into that module (defeating the 'pure, framework-free' testing benefit) or require refactoring it to return data structures instead of widgets (which is no longer zero-risk). Second, the `from tui_helpers import *` re-export strategy papers over the extraction rather than completing it — star imports are a well-known anti-pattern that breaks IDE navigation, makes it impossible to determine where a name is defined without reading the import chain, and will be flagged by any linter configured with no-wildcard-imports. If the goal is zero call-site changes, use explicit re-exports (`from tui_helpers import _feature_markdown, _escape_markup, ...`) so the module boundary is visible and auditable. Third, the action_* decomposition into 'pure decision/validation' vs 'thin dispatch' underestimates the coupling: most action methods read multiple pieces of PipelineApp state (self.current_board, self.features, self.selected_feature, self.config) and call self.notify/self.push_screen/self._refresh_features in interleaved sequences, not in a clean 'compute then dispatch' pattern. Extracting the 'pure' part would require defining a data class capturing all the state the function needs as input and returning a result object describing all the side effects to perform — which is essentially inventing a command pattern. That's a valid architecture but far from the 'simple middle ground' it was pitched as, and it should be acknowledged as a design decision with its own complexity cost rather than presented as an obvious simplification."
}
